Jove wrote:
> Leo wrote:
>
> >The other two (Orcrist and Sting) are utterly worthless,
>
>  That turns out not to be the case.  Sting is nice early on, especially
> in the mines.  Detect orcs when wielded is great.  If you can get to
> Expert in daggers Sting is easily worth it in the early game so you can
> survive to get a better weapon.  (Too much long term thinking can mean
> you won't ever get to the long term.)

Any class that can become Expert in dagger should seriously
consider naming the first blessed non-stacking elven dagger
Sting and wielding it some of the time.  It stays separate from
the quiver and still contributes for developing the skill.

>   At 6.0 base damage elven broadswords are almost the best one-handed
> weapons in the game.  (Only the katana can match it due to better damage
> vs large monsters.)  Elven broadswords are far more likely to turn up
> early on.

The weapon selection criteria for non-artifacts that monsters
use goes like this, trimming to only the ones I am interested
in during the early game:

runesword, katana, long sword, elven broadsword, broadsword,
scimitar, silver saber ... silver dagger, elven dagger, dagger,
orcish dagger, athame ...

It's notable that three of the sword skill classes come early
and are mixed.

>   For the late game, players ascend with Stormbringer.  Against many
> late game monsters Stormbringer is worse than Orcrist, BION.  Ditto for
> Fire Brand, Demonbane, Sunsword, etc..  (This is probably why no class
> can get to Expert with broadswords.  They're just too good of a weapon.)

Since Orcrist and Stormbringer share a skill class, that to
me means characters like to get Stormy will benefit the
most from Orcrist - Any chaotic.

For any lawful or neutral the only other artifact in the broadsword
class is the specialist Dragonbane.  Since there are *lots* of
artifacts in the long sword class this tells me that Orcrist is
as most an early game diversion for neutrals or lawfull.  Nice
on levels that still have orcs but bordering on throwaway later.
Okay to use but not worth spending a skill slot on.

>   So for early game survival they seem like a great combination.  Note
> that if you don't survive the early game, mid- and late-game
> considerations don't really come into it.  Note also that I've never
> actually tried Orcrist early on.  I have tried Sting and it is nice.

I have used Orcrist early on, but because of the reasoning above
only by chaotic characters.  Tastes great, less filling, for a
character
who expects Stormy at crowning.



